# Investigation: is removing iframe for the 4 cross-app launchpad routes
actually feasible? ## Context The LEAF launchpad (`/launchpad/`) uses
`leaf_header_preprod.js`'s router to hash-route between pages while keeping its
own header persistent. Most launchpad pages (Home, Find a Site, Form Library,
Our Impact, Voice of the Customer, Learning Center, Brand Guide) are fetched and
spliced directly into the launchpad's DOM (`iframe: false` / unset in
`NAV_SECTIONS`). Four routes are explicitly flagged `iframe: true` instead, and
mount in an `<iframe
  >` rather than being spliced: - Community of Practice —
  `https://leaf.va.gov/platform/CoP/report.php?a=launchpad_homepage` - Suggest
  an Idea — `https://leaf.va.gov/platform/ideas` - Privacy & Compliance —
  `https://leaf.va.gov/platform/privacy/report.php?a=resources` - Help Library —
  `https://leaf.va.gov/platform/help_library/report.php?a=homepage` The existing
  code comment explains why: "Full separate LEAF apps (CoP, Ideas, Help Library,
  Privacy & Compliance) don't survive that splice — wrong document, wrong
  scripts, wrong DOM." This was a deliberate choice, not an oversight. There's
  now a general preference (audit/architecture guidance, not a specific reported
  bug) to avoid iframe usage where possible. **Before any implementation work
  happens, we need to know whether removing iframe for these 4 routes is
  actually safe** — this is an investigation task, not a "go implement it" task.
  ## What to investigate, per app (CoP, Ideas, Help Library, Privacy &
  Compliance) For each of the 4 apps, using your visibility into their actual
  repos: 1. **Is it genuinely a separate, independently-deployed application**,
  or is it closer to a page within the same LEAF codebase that happens to live
  at a different path? (This changes the answer a lot — a page in the same
  codebase is a much smaller lift than a truly separate app.) 2. **Same-origin
  check.** Are these served from the same origin as the launchpad (`leaf.va.gov`
  / `leaf-preprod.va.gov`) with just a different path, or a genuinely different
  origin? (Same-origin doesn't eliminate the splice risk the code comment
  describes, but it does rule out CORS as a blocker for a fetch-based approach.)
  3. **Global JS state collisions.** Does each app load its own copy of shared
  libraries the launchpad also uses (jQuery, `LeafFormQuery`,
  `dialogController`, etc.)? Would loading two copies of the same library in one
  document break either app? Does either app rely on global IDs, `window.*`
  globals, or singleton patterns that would collide if two pages' scripts ran in
  the same document at once? 4. **CSS collisions.** Does each app ship
  global/unscoped CSS (e.g. rules on bare `body`, `#header`, `#footer`, or
  generic class names) that would leak into or be leaked into by the launchpad's
  own styles if spliced into the same document? 5. **Session/auth boundary.** Do
  any of these apps handle authentication/session state differently from the
  launchpad (different cookie scope, different CSRF token handling, different
  login flow) in a way that depends on being its own top-level document/origin
  context? 6. **Fragment-endpoint feasibility.** Could each app reasonably
  expose a stripped, content-only version of the relevant page — no `<head>
    `, no own header/footer, no full bootstrapping — specifically for splicing
    into another page's DOM? Is that a small change (a template variant) or does
    it require deeper refactoring of how each app renders? ## What NOT to do yet
    Do not implement any change to these 4 apps or to the launchpad's router.
    This is scoped to reporting back findings per app so a real decision can be
    made — attempting a splice-based fix without confirming points 3–5 above
    risks silently breaking either app's own functionality in ways that are hard
    to notice until a user hits the collision. ## Deliverable A short report,
    per app, covering points 1–6 above, plus a recommendation: safe to convert
    to fragment+splice, safe only with specific scoping work required (name it),
    or genuinely needs to stay iframed (name why).
